# Phase 031 — Implement `module ... end`, `export`, and the `import X as Y` alias

Two runs. The first run added the feature to the parser, the tree-walking VM
and the module files. The second closed the gap that left behind: the bytecode VM
could not run a single one of the programs the first run introduced. A review of
the second run then found four defects in it, all now fixed; what they were and
how each was caught is below.

## What changed

### First run — the language feature

| File | Lines | What |
|---|---|---|
| src/lexer.rs | +2 −0 | `("as", TokenKind::As)` (`src/lexer.rs:57`) and the `As` variant — `as` was lexed as an identifier, so `import json as J` parsed as three statements |
| src/parser.rs | +191 −14 | `Statement::Module { name, body }` and `Statement::Export { names, all }` (`src/parser.rs:224`, `src/parser.rs:230`); `parse_module()` (`src/parser.rs:1084`) and `parse_export()` (`src/parser.rs:1123`); both added to the statement dispatch; `as` accepted as an alias keyword beside the older `to` (`src/parser.rs:560`); `module_body()` (`src/parser.rs:267`) and `module_declared_names()` (`src/parser.rs:288`) — one rule for reading a module's names whichever of the two spellings the file uses |
| src/vm.rs | +316 −17 | `Vm::declare_module()` (`src/vm.rs:522`) — the module scope, the `export` check, the published members; the `Statement::Module`/`Statement::Export` arms; the rewritten `Statement::Import` arm (`src/vm.rs:1003`) — circular-import refusal, a builtin namespace is importable, the alias is a name for the module, an import does not take over a name the program already holds; `module_aliases` / `importing` / `declared_modules` / `module_depth`; `is_module_name()`, `declare_member()`, `qualified_member()`, `call_method` resolves an alias to the module it names; `load_module` binds a module file's functions under the same `module_function` name; `set_var` writes into the module's own scope above `module_depth` |
| src/analyzer.rs | +22 −4 | `Statement::Module` analyzed in a scope of its own, `Statement::Export` a no-op; both in `collect_later_names`; an import declares the module's own name as well as its alias |
| src/formatter.rs | +19 −0 | `Statement::Module` and `Statement::Export` arms (`src/formatter.rs:380`) — `module M … export … end` round-trips through `rb format` |
| src/linter.rs | +4 −0 | `Statement::Module` arm so unused-variable tracking covers a module body (`src/linter.rs:336`) |
| src/runtime.rs | +1 −1 | `module_bindings` reads `module_body` (`src/runtime.rs:56`), so a module file written with `module … end` binds the same names as one written without |
| modules/MathUtils.rb | +25 −21 | the `module MathUtils … export all end` wrapper its header comment has described since it was written |
| tooling/vscode/syntaxes/redblue.tmLanguage.json | +1 −1 | regenerated by `rb grammar` — the shipped grammar file must list the new keyword (`tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:102`) |
| tests/module_system_test.rs | +380 | 18 new `#[test]` functions |

